# Output that tells the truth: tabulate, summarize, histogram, boxplot, scatter
prompt: "Which command answers which question — when is `tabulate` enough and when do you need `summarize` or a histogram? What does a boxplot of wage income by sex show that a mean difference hides? Strengths and weaknesses: what can microdata's plotting commands do, and where do you have to export aggregates and plot elsewhere?"
next: {"title":"Why your output came back censored","href":"https://drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/dcast/microdata/why-your-output-came-back-censored.cast"}

## Question First, Command Second
voice: female
level: advanced

    text heading "Question first, command second" x 500 y 700 font_size 30 hidden true
    box root "1985 cohort: sex, wage" x 170 y 400 width 250 height 74 hidden true
    box q1 "How many in each group?" at.gap 110 at.offset [0,150] at.ref root at.side right width 280 height 70 hidden true
    box q2 "How large, on average?" below q1 gap 28 width 280 height 70 hidden true
    box q3 "What shape? Who is at the edges?" below q2 gap 28 width 280 height 70 hidden true
    box q4 "Do two variables move together?" below q3 gap 28 width 280 height 70 hidden true
    box cmd1 "tabulate" right q1 gap 90 #2f6b8f width 200 height 62 hidden true
    box cmd2 "summarize" right q2 gap 90 #2f6b8f width 200 height 62 hidden true
    box cmd3 "histogram / boxplot" right q3 gap 90 #b5482e width 200 height 62 hidden true
    box cmd4 "scatter" right q4 gap 90 #b5482e width 200 height 62 hidden true
    edge e1 root -> q1 curved hidden true
    edge e2 root -> q2 curved hidden true
    edge e3 root -> q3 curved hidden true
    edge e4 root -> q4 curved hidden true
    arrow a1 q1 -> cmd1 hidden true
    arrow a2 q2 -> cmd2 hidden true
    arrow a3 q3 -> cmd3 hidden true
    arrow a4 q4 -> cmd4 hidden true
    group map members ["root","e1","e2","e3","e4","q1","q2","q3","q4","a1","a2","a3","a4","cmd1","cmd2","cmd3","cmd4"] fit.h 560 fit.w 920 fit.x 40 fit.y 80 hidden true
    mark mark_shape target cmd3 kind box hidden true
    ```microdata md width 900 font_size 17 show above frame screen hidden true
    require no.ssb.fdb:54 as db
    create-dataset persons
    import db/BEFOLKNING_KJOENN as sex
    import db/BEFOLKNING_FOEDSELS_AAR_MND as born
    import db/INNTEKT_WLONN 2022-01-01 as wage
    generate byear = substr(born, 1, 4)
    destring byear
    keep if byear == 1985
    tabulate sex
    ```

Four short parts on making microdata's output tell the truth — and it starts by letting the question pick the command, never the other way round.
    draw heading

Everything hangs on the script we have built over six lectures: one birth cohort, everyone born in 1985, with their sex and their wage income.
    draw root

First question: how many people are in each group? That is counting, and counting is tabulate's entire job.
    draw e1 q1 a1 cmd1

Second: how large is the wage? One number per group — mean, median, quartiles — and that is summarize.
    draw e2 q2 a2 cmd2

Third: what shape is the income distribution, and who sits out at the edges? No single number answers that; you need a picture — a histogram or a boxplot.
    draw e3 q3 a3 cmd3

And fourth: do two variables move together, wage against education say? That is a scatter — blue answers in numbers, red answers in shapes.
    draw e4 q4 a4 cmd4

    pause 0.4

    clear

Take the first question to the machine: the datastore as db, an empty dataset called persons, then sex, birth year-month, and wage income as it stood on the first of January 2022.
    draw md md_line_1 md_line_2 md_line_3 md_line_4 md_line_5 parallel true

Then the population step from lecture six — year out of the birth string, make it a number, keep 1985 — and the question of the hour in one word: tabulate sex.
    draw md_line_6 md_line_7 md_line_8 md_line_9

Two rows, two counts. The figures here come from synthetic data, but the shape of the answer is the real thing: tabulate tells you who is in the room.
    draw md_out

    pause 0.4

    clear

Back to the map, and to the question everyone asks first: how big is the gap?
    show heading root e1 q1 a1 cmd1 e2 q2 a2 cmd2 e3 q3 a3 cmd3 e4 q4 a4 cmd4

Summarize answers it — one mean for men, one for women, and a difference you can put in a headline.
    highlight cmd2 effect glow

But two groups can share a mean and share nothing else, which makes the mean, all on its own, a rather mean summary.
    draw mark_shape

A boxplot splits each group into quarters, so the median, the middle half, and the high earners out in the tail all show up separately.
    point at.ref cmd3 gesture tap

These plotting commands are quick and they are safe — the server draws them and no individual ever crosses the wall — but they will not style: no panels side by side, no two series on one axis, no choice of colours.
    focus cmd3 cmd4

For anything fancier you collapse to a small table of aggregates, export that, and draw it in R or Python — which is where part four ends up.
    point at.ref cmd4 gesture circle

    quiz "You suspect a handful of very high earners is pulling the men's average up. Which output shows that?"
        intro: A quick check before we go.
        right: The boxplot — it shows the median, the quartiles and the outliers that a mean quietly absorbs.
        wrong: Both of those hand you numbers, and a number cannot show you a tail.
        * tabulate sex
        * summarize wage by sex
        + a boxplot of wage by sex

Ask the question out loud first, and the command picks itself: counts go to tabulate, one number per group to summarize, and anything about shape to a picture.

## Counting Heads, Measuring Tails
voice: female
domain: {"x":[0,1200],"y":[0,100]}

    ```microdata tab width 940 font_size 16 show above lines 8 frame screen hidden true
    require no.ssb.fdb:54 as db
    create-dataset persons
    import db/BEFOLKNING_KJOENN as sex
    import db/NUDB_BU 2022-01-01 as educ
    import db/BEFOLKNING_FOEDSELS_AAR_MND as born
    generate byear = int(born/100)
    keep if byear == 1985
    tabulate sex educ
    ```
    ```microdata sum width 940 font_size 16 show above lines 8 frame screen hidden true
    require no.ssb.fdb:54 as db
    create-dataset income
    import db/BEFOLKNING_KJOENN as sex
    import db/BEFOLKNING_FOEDSELS_AAR_MND as born
    generate byear = int(born/100)
    keep if byear == 1985
    import db/INNTEKT_WLONN 2022-01-01 as wage
    summarize wage
    ```
    text cmd "histogram wage" x 500 y 715 font_size 28 hidden true
    axes ax3 x_label "Wage income (1000 kr)" y_label People hidden true
    curve zero #8f887c expr "0" style.opacity 0.4 hidden true
    curve dist #2f6b8f expr 0.874*x*exp(-x/280) hidden true
    region tail x_from 900 x_to 1200 between ["dist","zero"] style.fill #f2c14e hidden true

Now that we have matched each question to its command, let's run them on the 1985 cohort — and see how one wrong choice of command puts a number in your abstract that describes nobody.
    draw tab tab_line_1 tab_line_2 tab_line_3 tab_line_4 tab_line_5 tab_line_6 tab_line_7

One new line at the bottom: tabulate takes categorical variables and does the only honest thing a category allows — it counts heads.
    draw tab_line_8

Every cell is a number of people, women and men split by education level, with no model and no average anywhere in between.
    draw tab_out

    pause 0.5

    clear

Now change the question from how many to how much, and the same skeleton carries a different payload.
    draw sum sum_line_1 sum_line_2 sum_line_3 sum_line_4 sum_line_5 sum_line_6

Wage income — INNTEKT_WLONN — as it stood on the first of January twenty twenty-two, because a wage figure belongs to a year, not to a person forever.
    draw sum_line_7

Point tabulate at this and you ask for one row per distinct kroner amount: tens of thousands of rows, no insight. A continuous variable needs summarize.
    draw sum_line_8

And back comes the whole variable on one line — how many people, what they earn on average, how far the extremes reach.
    draw sum_out

That mean is the number that travels. And wage income is precisely the variable that makes it misleading.
    highlight sum_out effect glow

    pause 0.5

    clear

So ask for the shape as well — one more line, histogram wage.
    draw cmd ax3 zero

Here is what a cohort's wage income really looks like: a crowd bunched up on the left, and a long thin arm reaching right.
    draw dist

The median stands where the cohort splits in two, around four hundred and seventy thousand kroner.
    line med dashed #8a5fa8 through [[470,0]] angle 90
    label l_med "Median ≈ 470" above-left #8a5fa8 attach_to med

The mean stands almost a hundred thousand further right, near five hundred and sixty — and nobody in particular is standing there.
    line mean_line dashed #b5482e through [[560,0]] angle 90
    label l_mean "Mean ≈ 560" above-right #b5482e attach_to mean_line

Everything between the two is made of this handful. In a wage distribution, the tail really does wag the mean.
    draw tail

One person on ten million kroner lifts the mean of a thousand people by ten thousand — and moves the median not at all.
    point at.ref tail gesture circle

That is the bargain: tabulate and summarize hand you exact numbers you can quote, while the histogram hands you no number at all — only the shape that says whether the mean deserves quoting.
    focus dist tail med mean_line

    quiz "A histogram of wage income leans left with a long upper tail. Where does the median sit?"
        right: Below the mean — the long right tail drags the average up past the middle person.
        wrong: The tail holds only high earners, so think about which of the two numbers it can pull.
        * Above the mean
        + Below the mean
        * Exactly at the mean

Counting heads takes tabulate and measuring takes summarize — but only the shape tells you whether one number is allowed to speak for everyone.
    mark mark_mean target l_mean kind box

## Nothing But the Mean
voice: female

    ```microdata md width 900 font_size 16 show code lines 7 frame screen draw.mode type hidden true
    require no.ssb.fdb:54 as db
    create-dataset persons
    import db/BEFOLKNING_KJOENN as sex
    import db/BEFOLKNING_FOEDSELS_AAR_MND as birth
    import db/INNTEKT_WLONN 2022-01-01 as wage
    keep if birth >= '198501' & birth <= '198512'
    boxplot wage by sex
    ```

Now that we have counted heads with tabulate and measured the tail with summarize, one question remains: the number you report as the gender wage gap depends on which command you ran.
    draw md md_line_1 md_line_2 md_line_3 md_line_4 md_line_5 md_line_6

Tonight's new line is just this — boxplot, the variable we measure, and the variable we split it by.
    draw md_line_7

Set the script aside; the whole lesson is in what it draws.
    clear

    path axis #8f887c points [[170,170],[870,170]]
    text t0 "0" #8f887c x 170 y 140 font_size 20
    text t400 "400" #8f887c x 403 y 140 font_size 20
    text t800 "800" #8f887c x 636 y 140 font_size 20
    text t1200 "1200" #8f887c x 850 y 140 font_size 20
    text xlab "Wage income 2022 (1000 NOK)" x 520 y 105 font_size 22
    text row_m "Men" #2f6b8f x 95 y 480 font_size 24
    text row_w "Women" #b5482e x 85 y 280 font_size 24
    parallel true

In our 1985 cohort, men's wage income averaged 690 thousand kroner, women's 515.
    dot mean_m #8a5fa8 at.x 572 at.y 480
    dot mean_w #8a5fa8 at.x 470 at.y 280
    parallel true

A gap of 175 thousand — 75 kroner per hundred. Two dots and the distance between them is everything a difference in means can say.
    path g_w dashed #8f887c points [[470,280],[470,380]]
    path g_m dashed #8f887c points [[572,480],[572,380]]
    arrow gap_arrow #8a5fa8 from.x 470 from.y 380 to.x 572 to.y 380
    label gap_label "mean gap 175" above #8a5fa8 attach_to gap_arrow

    pause 0.4

The boxplot gives five numbers instead of one. This box holds the middle half of the men: a quarter below 470 thousand, a quarter above 800.
    shape box_m #2f6b8f shape rect x 540 y 480 width 192 height 96

The line inside is the median — 605 thousand — the man standing exactly in the middle of the queue.
    path med_m #2f6b8f points [[523,432],[523,528]] style.stroke_width 4

The whiskers cover the rest of the bulk, from about 180 thousand up to 1.1 million.
    path wl_m #2f6b8f points [[275,480],[444,480]]
    path cl_m #2f6b8f points [[275,456],[275,504]]
    path wr_m #2f6b8f points [[636,480],[790,480]]
    path cr_m #2f6b8f points [[790,456],[790,504]]
    parallel true

And out here, drawn one dot per person, the handful of very high earners — which is where the tail wags the mean.
    dot out_m1 #2f6b8f at.x 840 at.y 480
    dot out_m2 #2f6b8f at.x 865 at.y 480
    label tail_label "very high earners" above #2f6b8f attach_to out_m1

The mean has to balance every krone these men earn, so it sits well to the right of the median; the median cannot feel them at all, because it only counts heads.
    camera center.x 700 center.y 470 zoom 1.7

    camera reset true

The women: median 500 thousand, and a box only two-thirds as wide, from 350 to 630.
    shape box_w #b5482e shape rect x 455 y 280 width 163 height 96
    path med_w #b5482e points [[461,232],[461,328]] style.stroke_width 4

Their lower whisker, though, runs almost to zero — a part-time year and a year of parental leave land in the very same variable as a full salary.
    path wl_w #b5482e points [[220,280],[374,280]]
    path cl_w #b5482e points [[220,256],[220,304]]
    path wr_w #b5482e points [[537,280],[660,280]]
    path cr_w #b5482e points [[660,256],[660,304]]
    parallel true

    erase gap_arrow gap_label g_w g_m

Measure median to median and the gap is 105 thousand: 83 kroner per hundred, not 75 — the same data, seventy thousand kroner apart.
    arrow med_arrow from.x 461 from.y 380 to.x 523 to.y 380
    label med_label "median gap 105" below attach_to med_arrow
    mark mark target med_label kind box

That is the strength of a box: five order statistics per group, no assumption about shape, and nobody's pay slip on screen.
    highlight box_m box_w effect glow

Its weakness is that a box cannot show how many people stand behind it, nor whether that bulk is one hump or two — a split distribution draws the identical rectangle.
    focus box_w

So: the mean, the whole mean, and nothing but the mean tells you the level of the gap; the box tells you its shape, and the shape is what tells you where the gap comes from.
    delivery grave

    quiz "The mean gap is 175 thousand, the median gap only 105. Why?"
        intro: One check before we move on.
        right: The mean must balance a few very large incomes among the men; the median just counts heads, so it barely moves.
        wrong: Look again at the two dots out past 1.1 million.
        * The women's box is narrower
        + A long right tail among the men pulls their mean up
        * The two groups have different sample sizes

## Plot Twist: Scatter and Export
voice: female
level: advanced
use: scatter_plot
with:
    x:
      - 812
      - 758
      - 690
      - 655
      - 641
      - 652
      - 640
      - 596
    'y':
      - 596
      - 540
      - 520
      - 515
      - 528
      - 478
      - 492
      - 478
    labels:
      - Oslo
      - Stavanger
      - Bergen
      - Trondheim
      - Tromsø
      - Ålesund
      - Drammen
      - Lillehammer
    fit:
      - 1
      - 0
    xlim:
      - 450
      - 900
    ylim:
      - 450
      - 900
    x_label: Men's mean wage (1000 NOK)
    y_label: Women's mean wage (1000 NOK)
    title: One dot per municipality — the 1985 cohort
    box: right

    ```microdata s font_size 16 show left lines 8 frame screen marks ["scatter wage wage_k","collapse (mean) wage, by(kommune)"] draw.mode type hidden true
    require no.ssb.fdb:54 as db
    create-dataset menn
    import db/BEFOLKNING_KJOENN as sex
    import db/BEFOLKNING_FOEDSELS_AAR_MND as birth
    import db/BEFOLKNING_KOMMNR_FORMELL 2022-01-01 as kommune
    import db/INNTEKT_WLONN 2022-01-01 as wage
    keep if birth >= 198501
    keep if birth <= 198512
    keep if sex == '1'
    collapse (mean) wage, by(kommune)
    // same lines again, sex == '2', wage imported as wage_k
    merge wage_k into menn
    scatter wage wage_k
    ```

Now that we have seen how a single mean hides the distribution behind it, one question decides whether you report a national gender gap at all: is the gap the same everywhere in Norway?
    draw s s_line_1 s_line_2 parallel true

Sex, birth month, municipality and wage income — the same four imports, for everyone born in 1985.
    draw s_line_3 s_line_4 s_line_5 s_line_6

Trim to the cohort, then keep only the men.
    draw s_line_7 s_line_8 s_line_9

Collapse to the mean by municipality, and the unit of analysis changes under your feet: about three hundred and fifty rows, not one person among them.
    draw s_line_10

The same again for the women, merged on kommune, so each municipality carries both means side by side.
    draw s_line_11 s_line_12

And one command plots it: scatter, men across, women up.
    draw s_line_13

Both axes in the same unit and the same range — without that, the picture below would mean nothing.
    draw axes title

Eight municipalities from the emulator's synthetic data: Oslo up in the corner, men at eight hundred and twelve thousand kroner, women at five hundred and ninety-six.
    draw point_1 point_2 point_3 point_4 point_5 point_6 point_7 point_8 parallel true

    pause 0.4

This is where the two means would be equal. Not one municipality is on the level.
    draw fit_line delivery grave

And the dots do not scatter randomly around it: Oslo's gap is two hundred and sixteen thousand, Tromsø's a hundred and thirteen — the richer the men's mean, the further the town falls below the line.
    highlight point_1 point_5 effect glow

A single national number would have averaged that slope away, and wage income being hours times pay, part of the distance is weeks not worked rather than kroner per hour.
    point at.ref point_1 gesture tap

All five commands of this series — tabulate, summarize, histogram, boxplot, scatter — ran where the data lives, nothing downloaded, nobody's row ever seen.
    draw s_mark_1

But you get the chart the command gives you: no colour by education, no confidence bands, no reference line — the equality line on this figure I drew myself, outside microdata.
    focus s

So the collapse is your exit: a few hundred means fit in the output window, and you rebuild the figure in R. The numbers travel; the people stay behind.
    draw s_mark_2

    quiz "Your journal wants this scatter with a fitted line, labels and error bars. What do you do?"
        intro: One last check before we close the series.
        right: You take out the aggregates — a few hundred means — and build the figure where you control every stroke; the individual rows never leave the server.
        wrong: microdata's scatter has no such options, and person-level data never leaves at all.
        * Export the person-level file and plot it at home
        + Collapse to municipality means, copy the table out, plot it outside
        * Run scatter again with more options

Four parts, one rule: let the question choose the command, and when the picture has to persuade, export the aggregates — never the people.

