    female|a||The line inside is the median — 605 thousand — the man standing exactly in the middle of the queue.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    female|a||The whiskers cover the rest of the bulk, from about 180 thousand up to 1.1 million.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    female|a||And out here, drawn one dot per person, the handful of very high earners — which is where the tail wags the mean.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    female|a||The mean has to balance every krone these men earn, so it sits well to the right of the median; the median cannot feel them at all, because it only counts heads.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    'female|a||The women: median 500 thousand, and a box only two-thirds as wide, from 350 to 630.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    female|a||Their lower whisker, though, runs almost to zero — a part-time year and a year of parental leave land in the very same variable as a full salary.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    'female|a||Measure median to median and the gap is 105 thousand: 83 kroner per hundred, not 75 — the same data, seventy thousand kroner apart.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    'female|a||That is the strength of a box: five order statistics per group, no assumption about shape, and nobody''s pay slip on screen.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    female|a||Its weakness is that a box cannot show how many people stand behind it, nor whether that bulk is one hump or two — a split distribution draws the identical rectangle.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    'female|a|grave|So: the mean, the whole mean, and nothing but the mean tells you the level of the gap; the box tells you its shape, and the shape is what tells you where the gap comes from.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    female|a||One check before we move on. The mean gap is 175 thousand, the median gap only 105. Why?:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    female|a||The mean must balance a few very large incomes among the men; the median just counts heads, so it barely moves.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    'female|a||Now that we have seen how a single mean hides the distribution behind it, one question decides whether you report a national gender gap at all: is the gap the same everywhere in Norway?':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    female|a||Sex, birth month, municipality and wage income — the same four imports, for everyone born in 1985.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    female|a||Trim to the cohort, then keep only the men.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    'female|a||Collapse to the mean by municipality, and the unit of analysis changes under your feet: about three hundred and fifty rows, not one person among them.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    female|a||The same again for the women, merged on kommune, so each municipality carries both means side by side.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    'female|a||And one command plots it: scatter, men across, women up.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    female|a||Both axes in the same unit and the same range — without that, the picture below would mean nothing.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    'female|a||Eight municipalities from the emulator''s synthetic data: Oslo up in the corner, men at eight hundred and twelve thousand kroner, women at five hundred and ninety-six.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    female|a|grave|This is where the two means would be equal. Not one municipality is on the level.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    'female|a||And the dots do not scatter randomly around it: Oslo''s gap is two hundred and sixteen thousand, Tromsø''s a hundred and thirteen — the richer the men''s mean, the further the town falls below the line.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
      voice: nb-NO-Chirp3-HD-Puck
    female|a||A single national number would have averaged that slope away, and wage income being hours times pay, part of the distance is weeks not worked rather than kroner per hour.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    female|a||All five commands of this series — tabulate, summarize, histogram, boxplot, scatter — ran where the data lives, nothing downloaded, nobody's row ever seen.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    'female|a||But you get the chart the command gives you: no colour by education, no confidence bands, no reference line — the equality line on this figure I drew myself, outside microdata.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    'female|a||So the collapse is your exit: a few hundred means fit in the output window, and you rebuild the figure in R. The numbers travel; the people stay behind.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    female|a||One last check before we close the series. Your journal wants this scatter with a fitted line, labels and error bars. What do you do?:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    female|a||You take out the aggregates — a few hundred means — and build the figure where you control every stroke; the individual rows never leave the server.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    'female|a||Four parts, one rule: let the question choose the command, and when the picture has to persuade, export the aggregates — never the people.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    '|a||Next: Why your output came back censored':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
      voice: en-US-Studio-Q
